# 64. IMPORTANT DEVELOPMENT RULES

Do not:

* Build unnecessary enterprise infrastructure
* Over-engineer the MVP
* Create dozens of features before validating the core workflow
* Assume data availability without checking
* Invent APIs
* Invent financial information
* Invent company announcements
* Hard-code one country
* Hard-code one AI provider
* Hard-code one exchange
* Make unsupported investment recommendations

Do:

* Keep architecture modular
* Keep costs low
* Cache aggressively where permitted
* Store structured events
* Cite sources
* Track dates
* Track historical changes
* Separate facts from interpretation
* Make the system observable
* Design for scale without paying for scale prematurely

---

# 65. MOST IMPORTANT PRODUCT TEST

For any company, the system should be able to answer:

> **"What changed?"**

Then:

> **"Why does it matter?"**

Then:

> **"Where is the company progressing?"**

Then:

> **"Where is it falling behind?"**

Then:

> **"What did management previously promise?"**

Then:

> **"Are those promises progressing?"**

Then:

> **"What is happening in the industry?"**

Then:

> **"What are competitors doing?"**

Then:

> **"What should I watch next?"**

If the system cannot answer these questions from reliable public information, it should say so rather than manufacture an answer.

